#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105458 :question/problem "![](images/d8/d8675cb0c59ba915cefe4b0bbe539f1e7e38b5a3ac2fae4bd2bb25e4de96e116.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{S} x^2y \\: \\textrm{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=\\sqrt2x-y$ and whose domain is a semicircle of radius $1$ centered at $O$ in the upper-half plane, as shown above." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S f(x,y,z) \\, \\textrm{d}S \n=\n\\iint\\limits_D f\\left(x,y,g(x,y)\\right) \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2} \\: \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x} = \\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(\\sqrt2x-y) = \\sqrt2,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(\\sqrt2x-y) = -1.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}y\\cdot \\sqrt{1+(\\sqrt{2})^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}y\\cdot \\sqrt{4}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2{∬}_{D}\\,{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r, \\theta),$ our domain can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\left\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 1, \\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\pi \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{∬}_{D}\\,{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A & =2{∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta  \\\\  & =2{∬}_{Δ}{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{∬}_{Δ}{r}^{4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,[\\frac{{r}^{5}}{5}]_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{15}{[-{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta ]}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{15}(1+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105459 :question/problem "Consider the surface integral of the function $f(x,y,z)=xz,$ where the surface $S$ is defined by $z=x^2-y$ for $0 \\leq x \\leq 2$ and $0 \\leq y \\leq 1.$\nThis surface integral can be written as a repeated integral, as shown below. \n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S f(x,y,z) \\: \\textrm{d}S \n=\n\\int_{0}^{2} \\int_{0}^{1} \\boxed{ {\\phantom{A A A A A A A}}} \\: \\text{d}y \\: \\text{d}x\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S f(x,y,z) \\, \\textrm{d}S \n=\n\\iint\\limits_D f\\left(x,y,g(x,y)\\right) \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2} \\: \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x} = \\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(x^2-y) = 2x,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(x^2-y) = -1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(2x)}^{2}+{(-1)}^{2}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}x({x}^{2}-y)\\sqrt{2+4{x}^{2}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\hspace{0.167em}{\\color{blue}({x}^{3}-xy)\\sqrt{2+4{x}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is ${\\color{blue} (x^3-xy) \\sqrt{2+4x^2}}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105488 :question/problem "Suppose $|\\,\\mathbf{a}\\,|=13$ and the angle between vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ is $\\theta = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}.$ Find the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a} & =|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\hspace{0.167em}|\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\  & =13\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =13\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{13}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105489 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle 3,3,-1 \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{b}=\\langle 2,-7,0 \\rangle.$ Find the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a} & =\\frac{\\mathbf{a}\\cdot \\mathbf{b}}{|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}+{a}_{z}{b}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{b}_{x}^{2}+{b}_{y}^{2}+{b}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\cdot 2+3\\cdot (-7)+(-1)\\cdot 0}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-7)^{2}+{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{15}{\\sqrt{53}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105490 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}$ and $\\mathbf{b}={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 \\\\ - 4 \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]}.$ Find the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a} & =\\frac{\\mathbf{a}\\cdot \\mathbf{b}}{|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}+{c}_{z}{b}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{b}_{x}^{2}+{b}_{y}^{2}+{b}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0\\cdot 2+0\\cdot (-4)+1\\cdot 0}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105560 :question/problem "Find the area of $\\triangle{ABC}$ below. Round your answer to two decimal places.\n\n![](images/dc/dc625408598aa3cbfda44ec877c9a26ab563dee9fcf757192fcf2cf8411f606a.png)" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the area of the triangle is \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A}=\\dfrac{1}{2}ab\\sin{C},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\angle{C}$ is between the sides of lengths $a$ and $b.$ \n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =CB=58 \\\\ b & =CA=40 \\\\ m∠C & ={31}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince \n\n$$\n\\sin{31^{\\circ}} = 0.515\\,038\n$$\n\nrounded to six decimal places,  we calculate the area as follows:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\mathcal{A} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 58\\cdot 40\\operatorname{sin}{31}^{∘} \\\\  & =1160\\cdot (0.515\\hspace{0.167em}038) \\\\  & \\approx 597.44,\\end{aligned}$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105561 :question/problem "Rounded to the nearest integer, what is the area of the quadrilateral $ABCD?$\n\n![](images/bc/bc9f32a9c0e8b042498a72e808a29274bd505e786d8ec43d0e0bc9888fdf6e44.png)" :question/worked-solution "The area of the quadrilateral $ABCD$ is the sum of the areas of the triangles $\\triangle ABC$ and $\\triangle ADC$ (see the illustration below).\n\n![](images/ac/acd2e923a65d0821cd59ed8b8274ceb5fc38def896467e37c8b1d454f958699e.png)\n\n First, we find the area for $\\triangle ABC,$ and get\n \n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{\\mathcal{A}}_{ABC} & =\\frac{1}{2}(42)(35)\\operatorname{sin}{36}^{∘} \\\\  & =735\\operatorname{sin}{36}^{∘} \\\\  & \\approx 432.022\\end{aligned}$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places.\n\nNow we find the area for $\\triangle ADC,$ and get\n \n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{\\mathcal{A}}_{ADC} & =\\frac{1}{2}(20)(18)\\operatorname{sin}{80}^{∘} \\\\  & =180\\operatorname{sin}{80}^{∘} \\\\  & \\approx 177.265\\end{aligned}$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places.\n\nFinally, we add together the areas of our two triangles to determine the area of $ABCD.$ We get\n \n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{\\mathcal{A}}_{ABCD} & ={\\mathcal{A}}_{ABC}+{\\mathcal{A}}_{ADC} \\\\  & =432.022+177.265 \\\\  & \\approx 609\\end{aligned}$$\n\nConsequently, to the nearest integer, the area of the quadrilateral $ABCD$ is $609.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105562 :question/problem "The area of $ABCD$ is $300\\, \\text{cm}^2.$  What is the area of $\\triangle ABC$ rounded to the nearest integer?\n\n![](images/c0/c06ffb123bdd24c3f83419767b6d1578223b8093089b79f91aae8f1fc6121305.png)" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by finding the area of $\\triangle ACD.$ We get \n \n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{\\mathcal{A}}_{ACD} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot DA\\cdot DC\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}D \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(25)(32)\\operatorname{sin}{70}^{∘} \\\\  & =400\\operatorname{sin}{70}^{∘} \\\\  & =375.877... \\\\  & \\approx 376\\hspace{0.167em}{\\text{cm}}^{2}\\end{aligned}$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer.\n\nThe difference of the areas of the quadrilateral $ABCD$ and $\\triangle ACD$ gives the area of $\\triangle ABC.$ Therefore, \n \n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{\\mathcal{A}}_{ABC} & ={\\mathcal{A}}_{ACD}-{\\mathcal{A}}_{ABCD} \\\\  & =376-300 \\\\  & =76\\hspace{0.167em}{\\text{cm}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105579 :question/problem "The area of ​​the triangle $\\triangle ABC$ is $24\\sqrt{2} \\text{ cm}^2.$ Find $AB.$\n\n![](images/e9/e93e732bd04cfda304c2098087548a638d8d869ccf8b1050235e8207825875d3.png)" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the area of the triangle is \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A}=\\dfrac{1}{2}bc\\sin{A},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\angle{A}$ is between the sides of lengths $b$ and $c.$ \n\nHere\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =AB, \\\\ b & =AC=12\\text{ cm}, \\\\ m∠A & ={45}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince \n\n$$\n\\sin{45^{\\circ}} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\n$$\n\nwe can calculate $c=AB,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot c\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\ 24\\sqrt{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 12\\cdot c\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{45}^{∘} \\\\ 24\\sqrt{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 12\\cdot c\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\ 24\\cancel{\\sqrt{2}} & =3\\cancel{\\sqrt{2}}c \\\\ 24 & =3c \\\\ c & =8\\text{ cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that $AB = 8\\,\\textrm{cm}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105590 :question/problem "Calculate the area of the triangle spanned by $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle 4,0,-3 \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{b}=\\langle 5,-1,2 \\rangle.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 4 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}4 & -3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(0\\cdot 2-(-3)\\cdot (-1))\\mathbf{i}-(4\\cdot 2-(-3)\\cdot 5)\\mathbf{j}+(4\\cdot (-1)-0\\cdot 5)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-3\\mathbf{i}-23\\mathbf{j}-4\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-3,-23,-4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the corresponding parallelogram is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+(-23)^{2}+(-4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{554}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of the triangle is\n\n$$\nA_{\\triangle} = \\dfrac{1}{2}A = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{554}}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105591 :question/problem "Calculate the area of the $\\bigtriangleup\\!ABC,$ if $A(1,0,-2),$ $B(-2,0,1),$ $C(1,1,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vectors $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ and $\\overrightarrow{AC}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(-2\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{k})-(\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-3\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-3,0,3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\mathbf{c}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{k})-(\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨0,1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ -3 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(0\\cdot 1-3\\cdot 1)\\mathbf{i}-(-3\\cdot 1-3\\cdot 0)\\mathbf{j}+(-3\\cdot 1-0\\cdot 0)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-3\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}-3\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-3,3,-3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ and $\\overrightarrow{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+{3}^{2}+(-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $\\bigtriangleup\\!ABC$ is\n\n$$\nA_{\\bigtriangleup} = \\dfrac{1}{2}A = \\dfrac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105592 :question/problem "Calculate the length of the altitude $CH$ of the $\\bigtriangleup\\!ABC$ if $A(-1,1,0),$ $B(1,1,2),$ $C(1,-1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude (height) can be related to the area of the triangle as follows:\n\n$$\nA_{\\bigtriangleup} = \\dfrac{b \\cdot h}{2},\n$$\n\n \nwhere $b$ is a base and $h$ is the corresponding altitude of the triangle.\n\nFor the $\\bigtriangleup\\!ABC,$ we have $h=CH,$ and $b=AB.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nA_{\\bigtriangleup} = \\dfrac{1}{2} CH \\cdot AB.\n$$\n\nSo, to find $CH,$ we first find $A_{\\bigtriangleup}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ and $\\overrightarrow{AC}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k})-(-\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨2,0,2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\mathbf{c}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k})-(-\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨2,-2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(0\\cdot 1-2\\cdot (-2))\\mathbf{i}-(2\\cdot 1-2\\cdot 2)\\mathbf{j}+(2\\cdot (-2)-0\\cdot 2)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =4\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}-4\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨4,2,-4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ and $\\overrightarrow{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $\\bigtriangleup\\!ABC$ is\n\n$$\nA_{\\bigtriangleup} = \\dfrac{1}{2}A = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\cdot 6 = 3.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $AB$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula $A_{\\bigtriangleup} = \\dfrac{1}{2} CH \\cdot AB,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB \\\\ 3 & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 3 & =CH\\cdot \\sqrt{2} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105593 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle 2,-3,2 \\rangle,$ $\\mathbf{b}=\\langle -1,0,5 \\rangle,$ and $\\mathbf{c}=\\langle 1,2,3 \\rangle.$ Find $\\mathbf{a} \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the cross product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\times c & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(0\\cdot 3-5\\cdot 2)i-(-1\\cdot 3-5\\cdot 1)j+(-1\\cdot 2-0\\cdot 2)k \\\\  & =-10i+8j-2k \\\\  & =⟨-10,8,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the scalar triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =⟨2,-3,2⟩\\cdot ⟨-10,8,-2⟩ \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-10)+(-3)\\cdot 8+2\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =-48\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105594 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle 4,2,-1 \\rangle,$ $\\mathbf{b}=\\langle 0,2,1 \\rangle,$ and $\\mathbf{c}=\\langle -1,-1,2 \\rangle.$ Find $\\mathbf{a} \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}).$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the scalar triple product $\\mathbf{a} \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c})$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ and $\\mathbf{c}.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}-2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+(-1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4\\cdot (2\\cdot 2-1\\cdot (-1))-2\\cdot (0\\cdot 2-1\\cdot (-1))+(-1)\\cdot (0\\cdot (-1)-2\\cdot (-1)) \\\\  & =20-2-2 \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105595 :question/problem "Find $\\mathbf{c} \\cdot (\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b}),$ if $2\\mathbf{a} \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times 3\\mathbf{c}) = 5.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor out the constants and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2a\\cdot (b\\times 3c) & =5 \\\\ 2\\cdot [a\\cdot (b\\times 3c)] & =5 \\\\ 2\\cdot 3\\cdot [a\\cdot (b\\times c)] & =5 \\\\ a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =\\frac{5}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{c}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot (b\\times a) & =-\\frac{5}{6},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand again we swap $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot (a\\times b) & =\\frac{5}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105596 :question/problem "Calculate the volume of the parallelepiped spanned by $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle 1,-4,2 \\rangle,$ $\\mathbf{b}=\\langle 0,-2,1 \\rangle,$ and $\\mathbf{c}=\\langle -1,0,3 \\rangle.$" :question/worked-solution "The volume of the parallelepiped spanned by three vectors is given by the absolute value of their triple product.\n\nFirst, we compute the triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}-(-4)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}+2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-2\\cdot 3-1\\cdot 0)+4(0\\cdot 3-1\\cdot (-1))+2(0\\cdot 0-(-1)\\cdot (-2)) \\\\  & =-6+4-4 \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the absolute value. So the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped is\n\n$$\nV = |-6| = 6.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105597 :question/problem "Find $k$ given that the vectors $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle k,2,1 \\rangle,$ $\\mathbf{b}=\\langle -2,-1,1 \\rangle,$ and $\\mathbf{c}=\\langle -1,1,2 \\rangle$ are coplanar." :question/worked-solution "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}k & 2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =k\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}-2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =k(-1\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 1)-2(-2\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot 1)+(-2\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot (-1)) \\\\  & =-3k+6-3 \\\\  & =-3k+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =0 \\\\ -3k+3 & =0 \\\\ k & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=1$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105598 :question/problem "Calculate the volume of the parallelepiped spanned by $\\overrightarrow{AB},$ $\\overrightarrow{AC},$ and $\\overrightarrow{AD},$ given the points $A(3,-1,2),$ $B(2,1,1),$ $C(-1,2,1),$ and $D(4,4,2).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the displacement vectors $\\overrightarrow{AB},$ $\\overrightarrow{AC},$ and $\\overrightarrow{AD}$ that span the parallelepiped:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(2\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k})-(3\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-1,2,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\mathbf{c}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(-\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k})-(3\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-4\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-4,3,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AD} & =\\mathbf{d}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(4\\mathbf{i}+4\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k})-(3\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}+5\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =⟨1,5,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the volume of the parallelepiped, we need to take the absolute value of the triple product of the above vectors.\n\nComputing the triple product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot (\\overset{\\to }{AC}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AD}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{vmatrix}-2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-4 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}+(-1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-4 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(3\\cdot 0-5\\cdot (-1))-2((-4)\\cdot 0-1\\cdot (-1))-((-4)\\cdot 5-1\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =-5-2+23 \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped spanned by $\\overrightarrow{AB},$ $\\overrightarrow{AC},$ and $\\overrightarrow{AD}$ is\n\n$$\nV=|16|=16.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105691 :question/problem "The closed curve $C$ is defined as $x^2+y^2 = 2.$ Find the outward-pointing unit normal vector on $C.$" :question/worked-solution "We can parameterize $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)  \n= {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x ( t ) \\\\ y ( t )\\end{array} \\right]}\n= {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}\\sqrt{2} \\cos   t \\\\ \\sqrt{2} \\sin   t\\end{array} \\right]},\\qquad\nt \\in [0,2\\pi)\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, positively oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector $\\mathbf r'(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $\\mathbf r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{'}(t) \\\\ {y}^{'}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}t \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ by $90^\\circ$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a $90^\\circ$ clockwise rotation is\n\n$$\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}0 & 1 \\\\ - 1 & 0\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nRotating the vector $\\mathbf r'(t)$ by $90^\\circ$ clockwise, we get\n\n$$\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}0 & 1 \\\\ - 1 & 0\\end{array} \\right]}\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- \\sqrt{2} \\sin   t \\\\ \\sqrt{2} \\cos   t\\end{array} \\right]}\n=\n {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}\\sqrt{2} \\cos   t \\\\ \\sqrt{2} \\sin   t\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of $\\mathbf r'(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation).\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n||\\mathbf r'(t)|| = \\sqrt{(-\\sqrt{2}\\sin t)^2 + (\\sqrt{2}\\cos t)^2} = \\sqrt{2},\n$$\n\nand since for our curve $x(t) = \\sqrt{2}\\cos{t}$ and $y(t) = \\sqrt{2}\\sin{t},$ it follows that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{n} & =\\frac{1}{||{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)||}\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}t \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}t \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105692 :question/problem "The closed curve $C$ is defined as $\\mathbf r(t) =  12\\sin t\\,\\mathbf{i} + 9\\cos t \\,\\mathbf{j}$ for $t \\in [0,2\\pi).$ Find the outward-pointing unit normal vector on $C.$\n\n*Hint: Observe that $C$ is traversed clockwise.*" :question/worked-solution "For convenience, we express $\\mathbf r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) \n= {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x ( t ) \\\\ y ( t )\\end{array} \\right]}\n= {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}12 \\sin   t \\\\ 9 \\cos   t\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, *negatively* oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector $\\mathbf r'(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $\\mathbf r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{'}(t) \\\\ {y}^{'}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}12\\operatorname{cos}t \\\\ -9\\operatorname{sin}t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented clockwise, we rotate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ by $90^\\circ$ *counterclockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a $90^\\circ$ counterclockwise rotation is\n\n$$\nR={\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}0 & - 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nRotating the vector $\\mathbf r'(t)$ by $90^\\circ$ counterclockwise, we get\n\n$$\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}0 & - 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{array} \\right]}\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}12 \\cos   t \\\\ - 9 \\sin   t\\end{array} \\right]}\n=\n {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}9 \\sin   t \\\\ 12 \\cos   t\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of $\\mathbf r'(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{n} & =\\frac{1}{||{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)||}\\begin{bmatrix}9\\operatorname{sin}t \\\\ 12\\operatorname{cos}t\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{9\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+12\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}}{\\sqrt{144{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t+81{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106038 :question/problem "Let $c = ⟨2, 1, 2⟩$ and $d = ⟨ - 1, 2, 2⟩$. Find $comp_{d}c + comp_{c}d$." :question/worked-solution "The scalar projections are\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{comp}_{d}c=\\frac{c\\cdot d}{|d|},\n\\qquad \\operatorname{comp}_{c}d=\\frac{d\\cdot c}{|c|}.\n$$\n\nHere $c\\cdot d=-2+2+4=4$, while $|c|=|d|=3$. Therefore\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{comp}_{d}c+\\operatorname{comp}_{c}d=\\frac43+\\frac43=\\frac83.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106040 :question/problem "The closed, positively oriented curve $C$ is defined as $\\mathbf{r}(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+4\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector on $C$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}=\\frac{a(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+b(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}}{||{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)||}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $a(t).$" :question/worked-solution "For convenience, we express $r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, positively oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}\\begin{bmatrix}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $a(t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106041 :question/problem "The closed curve $C$ is defined as $\\mathbf{r}(t)=7\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+24\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector for $C$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}=\\frac{a(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+b(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}}{||{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)||}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $b(t).$\n\n*Hint: Observe that $C$ is traversed clockwise.*" :question/worked-solution "For convenience, we express $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}7\\operatorname{sin}t \\\\ 24\\operatorname{cos}t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward normal vector of a closed, *negatively* oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{'}(t) \\\\ {y}^{'}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}7\\operatorname{cos}t \\\\ -24\\operatorname{sin}t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented clockwise, we rotate ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *counterclockwise* to obtain an outward normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise rotation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -24\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}24\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step $2.$\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{n} & =\\frac{1}{||{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)||}\\begin{bmatrix}24\\operatorname{sin}t \\\\ 7\\operatorname{cos}t\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{24\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+7\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}}{||{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $b(t)=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106042 :question/problem "The closed curve $C$ is defined as ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9.$ Find the outward-pointing unit normal vector on $C.$" :question/worked-solution "We can parameterize $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3\\operatorname{cos}t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}t\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}t\\in [0,2\\pi )\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, positively oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{'}(t) \\\\ {y}^{'}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\operatorname{sin}t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cos}t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation).\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n||{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)||=\\sqrt{(-3\\operatorname{sin}t)^{2}+(3\\operatorname{cos}t)^{2}}=3,\n$$\n\nand since for our curve $x(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $y(t)=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ it follows that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{n} & =\\frac{1}{||{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)||}\\begin{bmatrix}3\\operatorname{cos}t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}3\\operatorname{cos}t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106043 :question/problem "The closed curve $C$ is defined as $\\mathbf{r}(t)=4\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+8\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector for $C$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}=\\frac{a(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+b(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}}{||{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)||}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $a(t).$\n\n*Hint: Observe that $C$ is traversed clockwise.*" :question/worked-solution "For convenience, we express $r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, *negatively* oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented clockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *counterclockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise rotation is\n\n$$\nR=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}\\begin{bmatrix}8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $a(t)=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106044 :question/problem "Calculate the volume of the parallelepiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD},$ given the points $A(9,6,1),$ $B(9,7,2),$ $C(7,5,2),$ and $D(7,8,3).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ that span the parallelepiped:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(9i+7j+2k)-(9i+6j+k) \\\\  & =j+k \\\\  & =⟨0,1,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(7i+5j+2k)-(9i+6j+k) \\\\  & =-2i-j+k \\\\  & =⟨-2,-1,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AD} & =d-a \\\\  & =(7i+8j+3k)-(9i+6j+k) \\\\  & =-2i+2j+2k \\\\  & =⟨-2,2,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the volume of the parallelepiped, we need to take the absolute value of the triple product of the above vectors.\n\nComputing the triple product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot (\\overset{\\to }{AC}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AD}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{vmatrix}-1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0-((-2)\\cdot 2-(-2)\\cdot 1)+((-2)\\cdot 2-(-2)\\cdot (-1)) \\\\  & =2-6 \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ is\n\n$$\nV=|-4|=4.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106057 :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}12xyz\\,\\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=x+y$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+y)=1,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+y)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be evaluated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}12xyz\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,12xy(x+y)\\sqrt{1+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,12xy(x+y)\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{D}\\,12{x}^{2}y+12x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(12{x}^{2}y+12x{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[6{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+4x{y}^{3}]_{y=0}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(6{x}^{2}+4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\,[2{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}(2+2)-0 \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106060 :question/problem "Consider the surface integral of the function $f(x,y,z)=2xy,$ where the surface $S$ is defined by $z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ for $-1\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 2.$\nThis surface integral can be written as a repeated integral, as shown below. \n\n$${∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\boxed{\\phantom{AAAAAAA}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2x,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(2x)}^{2}+{(2y)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,2xy\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,2xy\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106061 :question/problem "![](images/36/365c6e6ebc861b9d8276773d5afe39b971096f0de6f07fced2c5a2ea9e035554.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}3x\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=x-y$ and whose domain is the quarter-circle of radius $2$ centered at $O$ in the first quadrant, as shown above." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x-y)=1,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x-y)=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}3x\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3x\\cdot \\sqrt{1+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3x\\cdot \\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{D}\\,3x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ our domain can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Δ}=\\{(r,\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le r\\le 2,\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{D}\\,3x\\,\\text{d}A & =\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{Δ}3r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\cdot \\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{Δ}3{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{2}3{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta [{r}^{3}]_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{3}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ]_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{3}(1-0) \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106062 :question/problem "![](images/78/7836860694ae6246dee36a70b86bc7fcd8cf7358d47a10586ea6880759fb1588.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=2x-y$ and whose domain is the circle of radius $2$ centered at $O,$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x-y)=2,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x-y)=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\cdot \\sqrt{1+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\cdot \\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{∬}_{D}\\,({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ our domain can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{6}{∬}_{D}\\,({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & =\\sqrt{6}{∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{∬}_{Δ}({r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\cdot r\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{[\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }4\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{6}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{6}[\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{6}\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106063 :question/problem "Consider the surface integral of the function $f(x,y,z)=-3xz,$ where the surface $S$ is defined by $z=\\sqrt{2}x+\\sqrt{2}y$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 2.$\nThis surface integral can be written as a repeated integral, as shown below. \n\n$${∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\boxed{\\phantom{AAAAAAA}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{2}x+\\sqrt{2}y)=\\sqrt{2},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{2}x+\\sqrt{2}y)=\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\sqrt{2})}^{2}+{(\\sqrt{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,-3x(\\sqrt{2}x+\\sqrt{2}y)\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,-3\\sqrt{10}({x}^{2}+xy)\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106064 :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\frac{z}{\\sqrt{1+2{cos}^{2}⁡(x+y)}}\\,\\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)$ for $0\\le x\\le \\pi $ and $-x\\le y\\le x.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y),\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be evaluated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\frac{z}{\\sqrt{1+2{cos}^{2}⁡(x+y)}}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)}{\\sqrt{1+2{cos}^{2}⁡(x+y)}}\\sqrt{1+2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y))^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)}{\\cancel{\\sqrt{1+2{cos}^{2}⁡(x+y)}}}\\cancel{\\sqrt{1+2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y))^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{-x}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)]_{y=-x}^{y=x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[x-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}]}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106085 :question/problem "![](images/30/3099b3b3932e30ebeb67b908e131aa296785dccf530d8f2076f23ce0cb819eaf.png)\n\nFind the area of $△ABC.$ Round your answer to two decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The formula for the area of the triangle is \n\n$$\\mathcal{A}=\\frac{1}{2}bc\\operatorname{sin}A,$$\n\nwhere $∠A$ is between the sides of lengths $b$ and $c.$ \n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}с & =AB=3, \\\\ b & =AC=7, \\\\ m∠A & ={128}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡{128}^{∘}=0.788\\,010,\n$$\n\nrounded to six decimal places,  we calculate the area as follows:\n \n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\mathcal{A} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 7\\cdot 3\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}{128}^{∘} \\\\  & =10.5\\cdot (0.788\\hspace{0.167em}010) \\\\  & \\approx 8.27,\\end{aligned}$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106086 :question/problem "![](images/7f/7f05c2f205b6ca3dd94bbe73f47dde73471ac25817381beb56431231f7ffe4dd.png)\n\nFind the area of $△ABC$. Round your answer to two decimal places.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/worked-solution "The sides adjacent to the $20^\\circ$ angle are $5$ and $13$. The triangle area is $$\\frac12(5)(13)\\sin20^\\circ\\approx11.11565466.$$ Rounded to two decimal places, the area is $11.12$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106087 :question/problem "![](images/98/98d0c5dfe759f744b361fe384c4c11c78e58ed6194086053b44076567081e986.png)\n\nRounded to the nearest integer, what is the area of the quadrilateral $ABCD$?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/worked-solution "Draw diagonal $BD$. In $\\triangle ABD$, $$BD^2=10^2+6^2-2(10)(6)\\cos62^\\circ\\approx79.66341.$$ In $\\triangle BCD$, the cosine rule gives $BD^2=9^2+CD^2-18(CD)\\cos51.8^\\circ$. The convex configuration shown selects $CD\\approx11.009953$; the other root gives a concave configuration. Add the two triangle areas: $$[ABCD]=\\tfrac12(10)(6)\\sin62^\\circ+\\tfrac12(9)(11.009953)\\sin51.8^\\circ\\approx65.42354.$$ Rounded to the nearest integer, the area is $65$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106088 :question/problem "![](images/98/98b3193783f0ac19bcb78035b133ad8dbf956528dfb7474606cc586d3bfecc34.png)\n\nThe area of $ABCD$ is $90{\\text{ in}}^{2}.$   What is the area of $△BCD$ rounded to the nearest integer?" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by finding the area of $△ABD.$ We get \n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{\\mathcal{A}}_{\\mathrm{△}ABD} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot AB\\cdot AD\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 16\\cdot 21\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}{56}^{∘} \\\\  & =168\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}{56}^{∘} \\\\  & =139.278… \\\\  & \\approx 139{\\text{ in}}^{2},\\end{aligned}$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer.\n\nThe difference of the areas of  $△ABD$ and the quadrilateral $ABCD$ gives the area of $△BCD.$ Therefore, \n \n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{\\mathcal{A}}_{\\mathrm{△}BCD} & ={\\mathcal{A}}_{\\mathrm{△}ABD}-{\\mathcal{A}}_{ABCD} \\\\  & =139-90 \\\\  & =49{\\text{ in}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106089 :question/problem "![](images/38/38004f5c93b047155973d32caa632c2c506d526c09ea891a7a02864997860f0e.png)\n\nThe area of $ABCD$ is $15in^{2}$. What is the area of $△BCD$ rounded to the nearest integer?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/worked-solution "The large triangle $ABD$ has area $\\tfrac12(12)(9)\\sin117^\\circ\\approx48.11435\\,\\mathrm{in}^2$. The concave quadrilateral is the part remaining after removing $\\triangle BCD$, so $$[BCD]=[ABD]-[ABCD]\\approx48.11435-15=33.11435\\,\\mathrm{in}^2.$$ Rounded to the nearest integer, the answer is $33\\,\\mathrm{in}^2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106094 :question/problem "Find $k$ given that the vectors $\\mathbf{a}=⟨k,1,1⟩$, $\\mathbf{b}=⟨5,0,4⟩$, and $\\mathbf{c}=⟨6,2,5⟩$ are coplanar." :question/worked-solution "Coplanarity means the scalar triple product is zero. Since $b\\times c=\\langle-8,-1,10\\rangle$, we obtain $$a\\cdot(b\\times c)=-8k-1+10=9-8k=0.$$ Hence $k=\\tfrac98$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106095 :question/problem "Find $t$ given that the vectors $\\mathbf{p}=⟨3,t,t⟩$, $\\mathbf{q}=⟨1,3,1⟩$, and $\\mathbf{r}=⟨1,1,3⟩$ are coplanar." :question/worked-solution "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot (q\\times r) & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & t & t \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}-t\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}+t\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3(3\\cdot 3-1\\cdot 1)-t(1\\cdot 3-1\\cdot 1)+t(1\\cdot 1-3\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =24-2t-2t \\\\  & =24-4t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot (q\\times r) & =0 \\\\ 24-4t & =0 \\\\ t & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $t=6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106098 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{p}=⟨\\sqrt{2},1,-1⟩$ and $\\mathbf{q}=⟨\\sqrt{8},-\\sqrt{3},1⟩.$ Find the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{p}$ onto $\\mathbf{q}.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{q}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{p} & =\\frac{\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q}}{|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{q}\\hspace{0.167em}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{p}_{x}{q}_{x}+{p}_{y}{q}_{y}+{p}_{z}{q}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{q}_{x}^{2}+{q}_{y}^{2}+{q}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{8}+1\\cdot (-\\sqrt{3})+(-1)\\cdot 1}{\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{8})^{2}+(-\\sqrt{3})^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{12}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-\\sqrt{3}}{2\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}-1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
